    <methods>
      <methodStep>
        <description>
          <para>Input. The CalCOFI Bottle Database as the calcofi_bottle ingest stages it: each bottle's temperature (T_degC) and practical salinity (Salnty) with their quality codes (T_qual, S_qual), the bottle's depth (Depthm) and its cast's position. A bottle whose temperature or salinity the provider flags 8 (suspect) or 9 (missing) gets no derived value; code 6 (data OK but taken from the CTD) and an empty code are good.</para>
          <para>Pressure. Sea pressure from the bottle's depth and latitude (gsw_p_from_z(-depth, latitude)).</para>
          <para>Spice (spiciness0, kg m-3): absolute salinity from practical salinity (gsw_SA_from_SP), conservative temperature from in-situ temperature (gsw_CT_from_t), then spiciness at 0 dbar (gsw_spiciness0; McDougall and Krzysik 2015), by the same function as the CTD derived products (ctd_spice()). Positive is spicy (warm, salty), negative minty. The key is the same as the CTD series'; filter on dataset_key to keep the two apart.</para>
          <para>TEOS-10 sigma-theta (sigma_theta_teos10, kg m-3): potential density anomaly referenced to 0 dbar, gsw_sigma0 of the same absolute salinity and conservative temperature (IOC, SCOR and IAPSO 2010). The bottle database's own sigma_theta was computed by the legacy DECODR software with the earlier (UNESCO 1983) equation of state and is kept, unchanged, in calcofi_bottle; the two differ by a median of about 0.004 kg m-3. Bottles where they differ by more than 0.1 kg m-3 are listed for the provider (questions.csv Q03).</para>
          <para>Withheld. A cruise whose input is known to be wrong gets no derived value until the provider answers: cruise 2021-05-3322, whose published salinity equals its own sigma-theta, so the salinity column holds sigma-theta (calcofi_bottle Q13; this dataset's Q03).</para>
          <para>Every rule is a function in calcofi4db (bottle_spice_sigma0(), which calls ctd_spice()) with unit tests that compare it to the Gibbs SeaWater toolbox directly.</para>
          <para>References. McDougall, T. J. and O. A. Krzysik (2015), Spiciness, J. Mar. Res. 73, 141-152, doi:10.1357/002224015816665589. IOC, SCOR and IAPSO (2010), The international thermodynamic equation of seawater - 2010 (TEOS-10), IOC Manuals and Guides 56. McDougall, T. J. and P. M. Barker (2011), Getting started with TEOS-10 and the Gibbs Seawater (GSW) Oceanographic Toolbox, SCOR/IAPSO WG127. Kelley, D., C. Richards and WG127 (2023), gsw: Gibbs Sea Water Functions, R package.</para>
        </description>
      </methodStep>
    </methods>
